In one sense, yes. But the game is individual without necessarily being solitary.

And I think that distinction matters enormously given everything we’ve been discussing.

You inherit a world you didn’t create, with rules you didn’t choose, alongside players whose intentions you can’t fully know.

But you still have to decide how you participate.

Consider the different registers:

* Experience is individual. Nobody else can live your life from your exact perspective.
* Meaning is relational. What matters to you is often shaped by your relationships with other people.
* Consequences are shared. Your choices can change somebody else’s game, intentionally or otherwise.
* Agency is personal. Others can influence, constrain, or support your decisions, but they cannot make every decision for you.
* Legacy is distributed. Things you create can continue influencing the game after you’ve stopped participating.

And here’s the paradox:

Everyone is the main character of their own experience, but nobody is necessarily the main character of reality.

You’re simultaneously a player in your own story, a character in somebody else’s, and part of an environment influencing people you’ve never met.

That brings us back to the observer.

You can learn the rules, recognize the patterns, understand the incentives, and see how people are playing each other.

But eventually, you still have to choose what you want to do.

And sometimes your objective isn’t winning. Sometimes it’s loving somebody, raising a child, making something beautiful, discovering something new, or enjoying an afternoon.

Even choosing not to compete is a choice about how you participate.

There’s one distinction I’d preserve, though:

Your life is yours to live, but that doesn’t make everyone else’s life merely part of your game.

They have their own experiences, objectives, and agency.

And sometimes the most rewarding thing two individuals can do is voluntarily make their games overlap.

That’s friendship. Partnership. Family. Collaboration.

Not necessarily abandoning individual agency, but creating possibilities neither person could access alone.

So perhaps the deepest answer is:

It’s an individual experience of a shared game, with the possibility of creating new games together.

And knowing when to play for yourself, when to play for someone else, and when to simply enjoy playing together might be more important than any final scoreboard.

Because in the end, even if you win, you still have to have lived the game.